const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "What are the best skills for engineers who don't want to code?",
    a: 'The strongest non-coding skill groups for engineers are product and requirements thinking, data fluency using SQL and BI tools without writing production code, technical communication and writing, and people-and-delivery skills like project coordination and stakeholder management. Combined with your engineering domain knowledge, these skills open roles like business analyst, product manager, solutions engineer, technical program manager, and technical writer.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can an engineer really succeed in a career without ever coding again?',
    a: 'Yes. Roles like business analyst, product manager, technical program manager, solutions engineer, patent agent, and technical writer do not require you to write or maintain production code. Many of them pay as well as or better than entry and mid-level software engineering roles once you have real proof of work in the skill group.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an MBA to move into a non-coding role after engineering?',
    a: 'Not always. Business analysis, technical writing, and solutions engineering rarely require one. Product management and technical program management can be entered without an MBA through an internal transfer or an Associate Product Manager (APM) program, though an MBA can help for management-consulting or leadership-track roles with a clear target function in mind.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which non-coding skill pays the most for engineers in India?',
    a: 'Product management and technical program management currently show the highest ceiling, with product managers at Indian product companies earning roughly Rs 20-40 LPA at 3-5 years and technical program managers averaging around Rs 24-35 LPA and higher at big tech firms. Business analysis with a CBAP or PMI-PBA certification and specialised API-focused technical writing also show strong mid-career growth.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does it take to build a non-coding skill and switch roles?',
    a: 'It depends on your starting point and the gap you need to close. Some engineers build enough proof for an internal transfer in a few months by doing the work informally in their current team first. Others need a longer stretch, sometimes closer to a year, especially for an external switch with no internal advocate. Give yourself as long as it genuinely takes to build one real proof piece, not an arbitrary deadline.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will AI replace non-coding roles like business analyst or product manager?',
    a: 'Partially, and unevenly. AI is already speeding up first-draft documentation, routine data queries, and templated reports. What stays with a human is framing the actual problem, deciding trade-offs and priorities, and being accountable for the call. Build toward that judgment layer instead of only the drafting layer.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the fastest way to test if a non-coding path fits me?',
    a: 'Do the work once before committing to it. Write one real requirements document, run one SQL-based analysis, or draft one clear technical explainer for something you already understand from your degree or current job. Show it to someone already working in that role and ask what is missing. That single test tells you more than any course description.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does becoming an engineering manager count as a non-coding path?',
